% ---------------------------------------------------------------- closing blocks
\newcommand{\booklegend}{%
  \begin{tcolorbox}[tkbase, unbreakable, colback=white, colframe=tkGray!60, title={\faBookOpen\enspace Books used in this plan}]
  \footnotesize\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}%
  \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}l X@{}}
  \LOB & Erik Helin, Adam Renberg. \emph{The little book about OS development}, 2015. The hands-on x86 guide. Free at \url{https://littleosbook.github.io}. \\
  \OSTEP & Remzi H.\ Arpaci-Dusseau, Andrea C.\ Arpaci-Dusseau. \emph{Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces}, v0.90, Arpaci-Dusseau Books, 2015. Main theory text. Free at \url{https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/}. \\
  \OSC & A.\ Silberschatz, P.\,B.\ Galvin, G.\ Gagne. \emph{Operating System Concepts}, 10th ed., Wiley, 2018. Reference theory text. \\
  \MOS & Andrew S.\ Tanenbaum, Herbert Bos. \emph{Modern Operating Systems}, 4th ed., Pearson, 2015. Hardware, I/O, design, security. \\
  \OSDI & Andrew S.\ Tanenbaum, Albert S.\ Woodhull. \emph{Operating Systems: Design and Implementation}, 3rd ed., Pearson Prentice Hall, 2006. MINIX 3 with full source listing. \\
  \end{tabularx}\par\smallskip
  The books do not come with this plan. \LOB and \OSTEP are free online; buy or borrow the other three (the roadmap lists their ISBNs).
  Page numbers are the \emph{printed} page numbers of these editions, not PDF page indices. With another edition, or with the free
  \OSTEP chapters (each one starts at page~1), find the reading by its chapter and section numbers.
  MINIX source references like \texttt{kernel/proc.c (07400)} give the file and its starting line in \OSDI Appendix B.
  \end{tcolorbox}}

